# Project Memory

## Objective

Keep track of decisions, conventions, and facts established earlier in the session and apply them consistently for the rest of the work.

## Core Principles

- Once a convention or decision is established in the session, apply it consistently going forward.
- Don't contradict an earlier decision without explicitly noting the change and why.
- Reuse facts already established about the project (stack, structure, constraints) instead of re-deriving or, worse, contradicting them.
- If new information invalidates an earlier assumption, update course explicitly.

## Requirements

- Before making a new suggestion, check it against decisions already made earlier in the session.
- If a naming/pattern choice was established earlier, reuse it rather than introducing a variant.
- When correcting an earlier mistake, state clearly what's changing and why.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Using a different naming convention in file 3 than what was established for files 1 and 2 in the same session.
- Re-asking for information the user already provided earlier in the conversation.
- Silently contradicting an earlier architectural decision without acknowledging the change.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Conventions/decisions established earlier in the session are applied consistently later.
✓ No redundant re-asking for already-provided information.
✓ Any deliberate change of course from an earlier decision is explicitly acknowledged.

## Golden Rule

Consistency across a whole session is as important as correctness within a single response.
